#!/usr/bin/env python3
# fafsa-deadline-seed -- generate the FAFSA filing-deadline seed migrations from
# the file a human actually edits, db/seed/fafsa-deadlines/deadlines.csv
# (RFC 223, slice fafsa/02/fifty-one-deadlines-typed).
#
# There is no machine-readable feed for state FAFSA deadlines: fifty-one
# agencies publish HTML pages and nothing else. So the corpus is HAND-TYPED, and
# brief 0010 D19 says hand-typed data lives in a committed CSV with a
# PROVENANCE.json, because hand-typed data has to be reviewable in a diff.
#
# A SIBLING OF bin/test-state-programme-seed AND NOT A GENERALISATION OF IT.
# RFC 223 discover/repo-grounding.md argues that at length; the decisive half is
# that the two files are not the same format. This corpus carries the
# publishers' own sentences, and those sentences carry commas ("March 2, 2027,
# 11:59 p.m. Pacific") and quotation marks, so it is RFC 4180 with QUOTED
# FIELDS. The sibling's naive comma split, and its refusal of any cell holding a
# double quote, cannot read this file at all.
#
# AND THAT ARGUMENT COVERS THE PARSING ONLY, which is worth saying plainly: the
# `-n`/exit-3 contract, one generated file per reading day, the append-only
# guard, reading a generated seed back as data, the ORDER append, and the SQL
# escaping are now COPIED here format-independently -- none of them knows what a
# row looks like. A FOURTH generator needing them is the trigger to extract them
# into bin/pyfunctions.py; this one is not, and the duplication is a decision
# rather than an oversight.
#
# WHICH IS ALSO WHY THIS ONE IS PYTHON. Quoted-CSV parsing and SQL literal
# escaping over publisher prose is exactly what the `csv` module and a careful
# escaper do correctly and what awk field splitting does not. bin/ already
# carries python scripts of its own and bin/pyfunctions.py is the declared python
# counterpart of bin/functions: the reserved usage exit codes, the log_* family
# that writes to stderr, and parse_short_options (stdlib getopt, short options
# only). Every bin/ convention holds here -- short options, all logging on
# stderr, NOTHING on stdout on a normal run, the reserved exit codes.
#
# THE CSV IS THE SOURCE; THE SQL IS AN ARTIFACT. Never hand-edit a generated
# seed: edit the CSV and re-run this. `-n` refuses a tree that differs from what
# this run generates, so a hand-edited or stale seed is caught rather than
# served.
#
# ONE SEED PER READING DAY, and the reading day -- not the award year -- names
# the file. That is what makes the corpus's own correction rule work.
# create-fafsa-filing-deadlines.sql states the rule: never hand-edit a generated
# seed, and a corrected row lands as a NEW generated seed upserting on the
# natural key rather than as an edit to a landed one. You cannot correct a row
# here without RE-READING its page, and re-reading its page gives it a new
# checked_on, which puts it in a new file.
#
# SO THE CSV IS APPEND-ONLY IN FULL, ROW BY ROW, AND THIS SCRIPT ENFORCES IT. A
# migration corpus is replayed from the top on every db-reset, so a landed file
# must never change -- which means the SET OF CSV ROWS CARRYING A LANDED READING
# DAY must never change. A row already in a generated seed may be re-stated
# identically (this script is re-run all the time and must be idempotent) and may
# be neither REWRITTEN nor DROPPED; the refusal names the file that holds it.
#
# AND IT NAMES THE WAY OUT, because the guard cannot tell a LANDED seed from one
# this same uncommitted run wrote ten minutes ago. If the named seed has NOT
# landed yet -- you are still authoring this reading -- delete it and its line in
# db/schema/ORDER, then re-run. The guard is about a LANDED seed: once a
# migration is in main it is replayed on every db-reset and may never change.
# That is printed by append_only_escape(), once per offending file, because the
# authoring run for this very corpus hit it on all 128 rows at once and had to
# work the answer out.
#
# AND THAT IS WHY THE IN-FILE NATURAL-KEY RULE IS PER READING DAY.
# fafsa_filing_deadlines_natural_key is (award_year, state, program, event) with
# NULLS NOT DISTINCT, and a correction is a SECOND CSV row carrying that same
# key with a later checked_on -- the two rows are two readings of one page, and
# the later seed upserts over the earlier one when the migrations replay. What
# may never happen is two rows with one key IN ONE READING DAY: they would land
# in ONE `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE`, which Postgres refuses outright
# ("cannot affect row a second time"). So the duplicate check below is keyed on
# (award_year, state, program, event, checked_on), which is the rule that keeps
# every generated file applicable. A file-wide uniqueness rule would instead
# forbid the very correction mechanism this corpus is built on.
#
# IT VALIDATES BEFORE IT WRITES, AND IT REPORTS EVERY FAULT IN ONE PASS. Each
# CHECK constraint on fafsa_filing_deadlines is mirrored here, so a typo is a
# REVIEW error naming its line number rather than a migration that fails at
# apply time in somebody else's db-reset. Each mirroring predicate carries a
# `MIRRORS <constraint>` comment, and bin/fafsa-deadline-seed-scripts-tests reads
# the constraint names out of the DDL and asserts this file mirrors exactly
# those -- so a thirteenth CHECK cannot go unmirrored in silence. A validator
# that stops at the first offender turns a five-line fix into five runs.
#
# A CITATION CARRIES EVIDENCE, OR IT CANNOT BE STORED (RFC 220,
# db/schema/require-a-check-on-every-external-document.sql). That migration
# dropped external_documents.url_status's DEFAULT and made url_checked_on NOT
# NULL, so `INSERT INTO external_documents (url) VALUES (...)` -- what this
# generator used to emit -- no longer applies to a migrated database at all. It
# refuses rather than backfills, because a made-up date is a check that never ran
# looking like one that passed (RFC 182).
#
# So the corpus carries its sweep: db/seed/fafsa-deadlines/link-status.csv, the
# answers bin/check-link got for every url the corpus cites, source and archive
# alike -- an archived address is a document like any other. It is a SOURCE file
# a human reviews in a diff, exactly like deadlines.csv, and it sits beside it.
# The mapping from an HTTP code to RFC 200's three words is the CALLER's and is
# the state-programme sibling's, verbatim:
#
#     2xx                     found
#     404, 410                not_found
#     everything else         unknown, WITH the date we asked
#
# A CITED URL WITH NO ROW IN THAT FILE IS A FAULT NAMED BY ITS CSV LINE. That is
# the same guarantee RFC 220 put in the schema, moved to where it can name the
# authored line rather than fail in somebody else's db-reset.
#
# THIS SCRIPT STILL TOUCHES NO NETWORK. The sweep is bin/check-link's job and an
# operator runs it; this script reads committed files and writes generated ones.
"""fafsa-deadline-seed: generate the FAFSA filing-deadline seed migrations.

Reads the authored corpus db/seed/fafsa-deadlines/deadlines.csv -- the
hand-typed, per-row-cited FAFSA deadlines a human edits and reviews in a diff --
and writes one seed migration per READING DAY into the schema directory, each
appended to db/schema/ORDER:

    <schema-dir>/seed-fafsa-filing-deadlines-<yyyy-mm-dd>.sql

The CSV is the source of truth and the SQL is a generated artifact: edit the CSV
and re-run this, never the other way round. Every CHECK constraint on
fafsa_filing_deadlines is re-checked here first, so a row that cannot be true is
named by its line number before anything is written, and a row a landed seed
already carries can be neither rewritten nor dropped.

It reads a SECOND source file beside the CSV, link-status.csv -- the sweep
bin/check-link answered for every url the corpus cites. external_documents'
url_status has no default and url_checked_on is NOT NULL (RFC 220), so a seed
states a citation's evidence or it cannot be applied at all; a cited url with no
row in that file is refused by its line in the deadlines CSV. This script itself
touches no network.

Usage:

    fafsa-deadline-seed [-c CSV] [-d DIR] [-n] [-h]

    -c CSV  the authored CSV (default: db/seed/fafsa-deadlines/deadlines.csv
            under the repo root). It names BOTH source files: link-status.csv is
            REQUIRED and is resolved BESIDE whatever this option names, in the
            same directory, never behind an option of its own. A run whose CSV
            has no link-status.csv beside it is refused and writes nothing.
    -d DIR  the schema directory to generate into (default: db/schema)
    -n      dry run: writes nothing, and exits 3 if the tree differs from what a
            real run would generate
    -h      help

The published USPS codes a state cell must be one of are read from
db/data/codebooks.json IN THIS REPO, whatever -c and -d name: the codebook is
this repo's own published reference data, not part of the corpus being
generated, and no option moves it.

Options are parsed by the stdlib getopt module, the same short-option grammar
every bash script in bin/ uses; there are no long options and no positionals.

LOGGING GOES TO STDERR, all of it, including the closing summary: this script's
product is files on disk, so a normal run writes NOTHING to stdout. The help
answering -h is the one thing that does.

Exit codes:

    0   the seeds and their ORDER lines are correct, or were written
    1   refused: a source file is not usable (the deadlines CSV, or the
        link-status.csv beside it, which is missing or unreadable), a cited url
        was never swept, or the corpus cannot be written
    3   -n only: the tree differs from what would be generated
    9   refused: db/schema/ORDER cannot be appended to safely (run bin/db-status)
    10, 11, 20, 22  usage errors
"""

import csv
import json
import re
import sys
import textwrap
from collections import Counter
from datetime import date
from pathlib import Path

# The shared python half of bin/functions (bin/pyfunctions.py): the EXIT_* band,
# the log_* family that writes to stderr, fatal, and the post-getopt guard
# suite. Resolved against THIS script's own directory rather than the caller's
# cwd, because these scripts are run from anywhere -- and with no bytecode cache
# beside it, because bin/ is a reviewed directory of scripts and not a package
# build tree.
sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))
from pyfunctions import (  # noqa: E402  (import after the sys.path bootstrap above)
    EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE,
    fatal as shared_fatal,
    log_error,
    log_info,
    parse_short_options,
    write_atomically,
)

SCRIPT_NAME = "fafsa-deadline-seed"


# Every refusal names the script, because this one runs under bin/shell-tests
# and beside three other seed generators: a bare "[FATAL] ORDER cannot be
# appended to" costs the reader a lookup to find out which one said it.
def fatal(message, status=1):
    shared_fatal(f"{SCRIPT_NAME}: {message}", status=status)


REPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
DEFAULT_CSV = REPO_ROOT / "db" / "seed" / "fafsa-deadlines" / "deadlines.csv"
DEFAULT_SCHEMA_DIR = REPO_ROOT / "db" / "schema"
CODEBOOKS = REPO_ROOT / "db" / "data" / "codebooks.json"

# Two exit codes bin/functions reserves that bin/pyfunctions.py does not carry,
# because no python script in bin/ has needed them before. The NUMBERS are
# bin/functions' and the names are its names, so this file cannot disagree with
# its bash siblings about what a 3 or a 9 means:
#   EXIT_SEED_WOULD_CHANGE  3  -n: the tree is not what this corpus generates
#   EXIT_SCHEMA_ORDER_DRIFT 9  ORDER and the schema directory disagree
EXIT_SEED_WOULD_CHANGE = 3
EXIT_SCHEMA_ORDER_DRIFT = 9

GETOPT_SPEC = "c:d:nh"

# The header this generator was written against, exactly and in order. A CSV
# with a different header is REFUSED rather than read positionally: a reordered
# column would silently move an archive url into a source url, or one
# publisher's words onto another's deadline.
#
# ONE declaration, on ONE line, because there is one authored file -- and
# bin/fafsa-deadline-seed-scripts-tests READS THIS LINE to derive its fixtures.
# A harness that retyped the header could go on asserting the refusal of a
# header this script had stopped expecting.
EXPECTED_HEADER = "state,award_year,program,event,deadline_kind,deadline_date,deadline_as_published,publisher,source_url,archive_url,checked_on,expires_on"

# The SWEPT file, declared once and read out of these lines by the harness
# rather than retyped there. IT IS A HAND-COMMITTED SOURCE FILE, not a generated
# one: declared once, and read out of these lines
# by the harness rather than retyped there. The header is the column order
# external_documents itself names them in, and is the sibling's
# (db/seed/test-state-programmes/link-status.csv), because the two files answer
# the same question about the same table.
#
# It sits BESIDE the authored CSV rather than behind an option of its own: the
# sweep is a fact about THAT corpus's urls, so naming one file names both, and a
# harness fixture in a temp tree gets its own sweep for free.
LINK_STATUS_NAME = "link-status.csv"
EXPECTED_LINK_STATUS_HEADER = "url,url_status,url_checked_on"

# RFC 200's three words, which is also the link_status DOMAIN's whole range.
LINK_STATUSES = ("found", "not_found", "unknown")

# The GENERATED file name, with the reading day in it, declared once and read by
# the harness for the same reason as the header.
SEED_NAME_TEMPLATE = "seed-fafsa-filing-deadlines-%s.sql"
SEED_NAME_PATTERN = re.compile(r"^seed-fafsa-filing-deadlines-(\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2})\.sql$")

# THE INDENTS OF A GENERATED SEED ARE A CONTRACT BETWEEN THE WRITER AND THE
# READER, so they are named once here and never typed again.
#
# A deadline tuple is indented TUPLE_INDENT and an external_documents row
# DOCUMENT_INDENT, and read_prior_seeds tells the two apart by exactly that
# difference: DOCUMENT_INDENT begins with TUPLE_INDENT and is longer, so a line
# starting with TUPLE_INDENT + "(" is a deadline tuple and nothing else.
#
# THE APPEND-ONLY GUARD'S CORRECTNESS DEPENDS ON THE WRITER AND THE READER
# AGREEING. When these were three magic strings spread over both halves, making
# the generated SQL prettier by a space would have made read_prior_seeds match
# nothing -- and a guard that reads no landed tuples does not fail: it reports
# every landed row as DROPPED, whose printed remedy is to delete a landed
# migration. Change the indent HERE, once, or the guard silently stops being one.
TUPLE_INDENT = "  "
DOCUMENT_INDENT = "    "

# The four AidDeadlineEvent values and the three deadline kinds, as the DDL's
# CHECK lists them. Not derived from the Kotlin enum: this script cannot compile
# Kotlin.
#
# THESE TWO LINES ARE SWEPT BY THE HARNESS AGAINST THE DDL ITSELF, member for
# member and not merely by constraint name. A name sweep proves the mirror EXISTS
# and says nothing about what is IN it, so a value renamed in
# create-fafsa-filing-deadlines.sql would leave this generator happily admitting
# a row PostgreSQL refuses at apply time -- in somebody else's db-reset, which is
# the whole failure this script exists to prevent. The sweep reads the members
# out of `CHECK (event IN (...))` and `CHECK (deadline_kind IN (...))` and
# asserts SET EQUALITY with these tuples, so they are written on ONE line each
# and quoted plainly, for the same reason EXPECTED_HEADER is.
EVENTS = ("form_opens", "priority_deadline", "final_deadline", "corrections_deadline")
KINDS = ("date", "wording", "none")

# The `academic_year` DOMAIN's range (0087, RFC 170 D14). award_year is a START
# year: 2027 IS the 2027-28 FAFSA, and the "2027-28" label is rendered by
# AcademicYear at read time and never stored.
#
# THE TWO NUMBERS ARE SWEPT AGAINST THE DOMAIN'S OWN CHECK by the harness, on the
# same argument as EVENTS and KINDS above: the `# MIRRORS academic_year_check`
# tag proves only that a constraint of that name is declared somewhere in
# db/schema, and a domain narrowed there would leave this generator admitting a
# year the database refuses.
AWARD_YEAR_MIN = 2000
AWARD_YEAR_MAX = 2100

ISO_DATE = re.compile(r"^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$")
URL_SHAPE = re.compile(r"^https?://")
STATE_SHAPE = re.compile(r"^[A-Z]{2}$")
# Printable ASCII, and nothing else. See first_byte_offence below.
PRINTABLE_ASCII = re.compile(r"^[ -~]*$")


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Small readers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def published_state_codes(path=CODEBOOKS):
    """The 59 USPS codes db/data/codebooks.json publishes.

    THE PATH IS A DEFAULT ARGUMENT AND NOT A MODULE GLOBAL READ INSIDE, so a
    caller -- the harness above all -- can point this at its own fixture. Reading
    CODEBOOKS directly made this the ONE input -c and -d could not route: a
    harness that believes every run is hermetic in a throwaway temp tree was in
    fact opening the operator's real codebook, and would have gone on passing if
    that file changed under it. The DEFAULT is still the repo's own file, which
    is what every real run wants and what -h now says.

    MEMBERSHIP, not shape. `^[A-Z]{2}$` admits 676 strings and 617 of them name
    nothing, so the DDL's regex is a coarse backstop and the real check is here
    and in FafsaFilingDeadlineSeedTest -- which reads THIS file, the same one
    AdmissionTestStateProgrammeSeedTest asserts against. The table carries no
    foreign key into us_states yet, because that table is filled by the codebook
    ingest and is empty in a freshly migrated database
    (create-fafsa-filing-deadlines.sql says so at its `state` column), so
    nothing downstream would catch 'ZZ'."""
    try:
        document = json.loads(Path(path).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        codes = {row["code"] for row in document["us_states"]["codes"]}
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError) as error:
        fatal(
            f"could not read the published USPS codes out of [{path}] "
            f"([{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]); membership cannot be checked, so "
            f"nothing was written"
        )
    if not codes:
        fatal(f"[{path}] publishes no us_states codes at all; nothing was written")
    return codes


def is_iso_date(text):
    """A real day, and not merely a yyyy-mm-dd shape. A pattern-only check hands
    2027-02-30 to Postgres as a literal that fails at apply time in somebody
    else's db-reset."""
    if not ISO_DATE.match(text):
        return False
    try:
        date.fromisoformat(text)
    except ValueError:
        return False
    return True


def cycle_opens_on(award_year):
    """The SQL function fafsa_cycle_opens_on, in python: 1 July of the year
    BEFORE the award year.

    It is `award_year - 1` and never `award_year - 2`. The 2027-28 FAFSA opens
    in October 2026, so nothing about that cycle -- no deadline, no reading of a
    page -- belongs before that summer, and the bound is what refuses LAST
    cycle's 2 March priority deadline typed under THIS cycle's key.

    THE ARITHMETIC IS SWEPT AGAINST THE SQL FUNCTION by the harness -- the
    offset, the month and the day -- because the DDL's own comment calls
    fafsa_cycle_opens_on "one home" for a bound two CHECKs ask for, and this
    line is a SECOND home that no name-level tag could hold to it."""
    return date(award_year - 1, 7, 1)


def cycle_closes_on(award_year):
    """The upper half of the window: 31 December of the year AFTER the award
    year, which admits the corrections deadline that falls eleven months after
    the next cycle's form opened.

    The DDL writes this half inline, as `make_date(award_year::INT + 1, 12, 31)`
    in fafsa_filing_deadlines_date_in_cycle_check, and the harness sweeps this
    arithmetic against it exactly as it does the lower bound."""
    return date(award_year + 1, 12, 31)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SQL rendering
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def sql_quoted(text):
    """A SQL string literal: the apostrophes doubled and nothing else.

    That is the WHOLE escaper, and it is correct only while
    standard_conforming_strings is on and the cell carries no backslash and no
    byte outside printable ASCII. Both are refused by the validator, by line
    number, before any cell reaches this function."""
    doubled = text.replace("'", "''")
    return f"'{doubled}'"


def sql_text_or_null(text):
    return "NULL" if text == "" else sql_quoted(text)


def sql_date(text):
    return f"DATE {sql_quoted(text)}"


def document_id(url):
    """The document id of a url, as a SUBSELECT rather than a literal. The id is
    a uuidv7 the DATABASE mints, so a generated file cannot know it: the row
    that cites a document looks it up by the one thing both statements agree on,
    which is the url -- the natural key external_documents is built on."""
    return f"(SELECT id FROM external_documents WHERE url = {sql_quoted(url)})"


def document_id_or_null(url):
    return "NULL" if url == "" else document_id(url)


# THE TWELVE COLUMNS OF A DEADLINE TUPLE, IN ORDER, EACH WITH THE RENDERER OF ITS
# CELL -- and this is the ONE place that order is stated.
#
# It used to be stated three times: the INSERT's column list, the hand-written
# VALUES f-string, and read_prior_seeds' positional fields[0..3]. NINE of the
# twelve columns are TEXT or DATE, so swapping `program` with `publisher`, or
# `checked_on` with `expires_on`, produces a migration that APPLIES CLEANLY and
# stores wrong data for every row -- and nothing downstream would catch it: the
# :db seed test reads the CSV and never queries the table, and this generator's
# harness runs with no PostgreSQL at all.
#
# So all three sites DERIVE from this tuple: insert_column_lines() writes the
# column list, render_tuple() writes the VALUES, and KEY_INDEXES gives the reader
# the positions of the natural key. The harness asserts the three agree.
SEED_COLUMNS = (
    ("award_year", lambda cells: str(cells["award_year"])),
    ("state", lambda cells: sql_text_or_null(cells["state"])),
    ("program", lambda cells: sql_quoted(cells["program"])),
    ("event", lambda cells: sql_quoted(cells["event"])),
    ("deadline_kind", lambda cells: sql_quoted(cells["kind"])),
    ("deadline_date", lambda cells: sql_date(cells["deadline_date"]) if cells["deadline_date"] != "" else "NULL"),
    ("deadline_as_published", lambda cells: sql_quoted(cells["as_published"])),
    ("publisher", lambda cells: sql_quoted(cells["publisher"])),
    ("source_document_id", lambda cells: document_id(cells["source_url"])),
    ("archive_document_id", lambda cells: document_id_or_null(cells["archive_url"])),
    ("checked_on", lambda cells: sql_date(cells["checked_on"])),
    ("expires_on", lambda cells: sql_date(cells["expires_on"])),
)
SEED_COLUMN_NAMES = tuple(name for name, _ in SEED_COLUMNS)

# The natural key fafsa_filing_deadlines_natural_key is built on, as POSITIONS in
# the tuple above rather than as the literals 0..3 read_prior_seeds used to carry.
KEY_COLUMNS = ("award_year", "state", "program", "event")
KEY_INDEXES = tuple(SEED_COLUMN_NAMES.index(name) for name in KEY_COLUMNS)

# THE SHAPE OF THE GENERATED STATEMENT, written down once and read back by
# deadline_tuple_lines: the reader walks to this INSERT, past its column list, to
# its VALUES, and then reads tuples until the ON CONFLICT line. The writer below
# emits these very strings, so the reader cannot be looking for a statement the
# writer stopped writing.
DEADLINE_INSERT = "INSERT INTO fafsa_filing_deadlines"
VALUES_LINE = "VALUES"
CONFLICT_PREFIX = "ON CONFLICT "

# The word a message uses for the row whose `state` is SQL NULL. The column is
# nullable precisely so that "federal" is not a state code; the label is a
# rendering and never a value.
FEDERAL_LABEL = "federal"


def jurisdiction(state_cell):
    """The jurisdiction a `state` cell names: the USPS code, or None for the
    federal rows -- the value the nullable column itself carries. The empty
    string is the CSV's spelling of it and stops here, so that a key read back
    out of a landed seed (SQL NULL -> None) and a key rendered from the CSV are
    the same python value."""
    return None if state_cell == "" else state_cell


def jurisdiction_label(value):
    return FEDERAL_LABEL if value is None or value == "" else value


def insert_column_lines():
    """The INSERT's column list, wrapped as the generated file has always wrapped
    it, and DERIVED from SEED_COLUMNS rather than retyped beside it."""
    wrapped = textwrap.wrap(
        ", ".join(SEED_COLUMN_NAMES),
        width=76,
        initial_indent="    (",
        subsequent_indent="     ",
        break_long_words=False,
        break_on_hyphens=False,
    )
    return wrapped[:-1] + [wrapped[-1] + ")"]


def render_tuple(cells):
    """One VALUES tuple, one line, in SEED_COLUMNS' order."""
    return f"{TUPLE_INDENT}(" + ", ".join(render(cells) for _, render in SEED_COLUMNS) + ")"


def tuple_fields(line):
    """The rendered fields of a tuple LINE -- the writer's own output, or a line
    read back out of a generated seed -- or None if the line is not a tuple.

    The trailing comma a VALUES list puts between tuples is not part of the
    tuple, and neither is the indent."""
    text = line.rstrip()
    if text.endswith(","):
        text = text[:-1]
    if not text.startswith(TUPLE_INDENT + "(") or text.startswith(DOCUMENT_INDENT + "("):
        return None
    if not text.endswith(")"):
        return None
    return split_sql_fields(text[len(TUPLE_INDENT) + 1 : -1])


def split_sql_fields(text):
    """The top-level comma-separated fields of a rendered tuple's interior.

    Used to read a LANDED seed back, for the append-only guard. A plain
    `text.split(",")` cannot do it: a publisher's sentence carries commas inside
    its quoted literal, and a subselected document id carries them inside
    parentheses. This walks the string once, tracking quote state (with ''
    escapes) and paren depth, which is exactly the grammar this file writes."""
    fields = []
    current = []
    depth = 0
    in_string = False
    index = 0
    while index < len(text):
        character = text[index]
        if in_string:
            if character == "'":
                if text[index + 1 : index + 2] == "'":
                    current.append("''")
                    index += 2
                    continue
                in_string = False
            current.append(character)
        elif character == "'":
            in_string = True
            current.append(character)
        elif character == "(":
            depth += 1
            current.append(character)
        elif character == ")":
            depth -= 1
            current.append(character)
        elif character == "," and depth == 0:
            fields.append("".join(current).strip())
            current = []
        else:
            current.append(character)
        index += 1
    fields.append("".join(current).strip())
    return fields


def sql_unquoted(field):
    """The python value of a rendered literal: NULL, or a quoted string with its
    doubled apostrophes folded back."""
    if field == "NULL":
        return None
    if field.startswith("'") and field.endswith("'") and len(field) >= 2:
        return field[1:-1].replace("''", "'")
    return field


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The authored CSV
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def first_byte_offence(line):
    """The first character of `line` that no SQL literal this script writes may
    carry: its 1-based column, the character itself, and the rule it broke -- or
    None if the line is clean.

    NAMED, and not merely detected. The cells here are publishers' sentences
    hundreds of characters long, and the two offences are both invisible in an
    editor: a smart quote looks like a quote and a CR looks like nothing at all.
    A refusal that says only "a byte that is not printable ASCII, or a
    backslash" leaves the operator to work out WHICH rule fired and WHERE, by
    eye, on a line they cannot see the offender in."""
    for column, character in enumerate(line, start=1):
        if character == "\\":
            return column, character, "a backslash"
        if not PRINTABLE_ASCII.match(character):
            return column, character, "a byte that is not printable ASCII"
    return None


def shown_character(character):
    """The offending character as a message can print it: itself when it is
    visible, and its python escape when it is not -- a CR printed raw would move
    the cursor rather than appear."""
    if PRINTABLE_ASCII.match(character):
        return character
    return repr(character)[1:-1]


def read_rows(csv_path, csv_label, faults):
    """Every record of the CSV, as (line number, raw text, [cells]).

    PER PHYSICAL LINE, on purpose. RFC 4180 lets a quoted field carry a newline;
    this corpus refuses one, because a cell holding a newline is not printable
    ASCII and would be a SQL literal nobody can review in a diff. Parsing line
    by line is therefore exact AND it gives every fault the line number a human
    can go and fix, which csv.reader's own line_num cannot promise once a record
    spans lines.

    The bytes are read raw and checked BEFORE they are decoded: a smart quote
    pasted off a state page, a tab, a CR from a CRLF checkout and a backslash are
    all named by their line rather than escaped into a literal whose meaning
    depends on a server setting."""
    try:
        raw = csv_path.read_bytes()
    except OSError as error:
        fatal(f"could not read [{csv_label}]: [{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]")

    chunks = raw.split(b"\n")
    records = []
    for index, chunk in enumerate(chunks, start=1):
        # A trailing newline ends the last record; it is not an empty record.
        if index == len(chunks) and chunk == b"":
            break
        # latin-1 never fails, so EVERY byte survives to be judged by the rule
        # below rather than dying in a decoder with a traceback naming a byte
        # offset instead of a line.
        line = chunk.decode("latin-1")
        offence = first_byte_offence(line)
        if offence is not None:
            column, character, rule = offence
            # THE BYTE AND ITS COLUMN, because the line is a publisher's
            # sentence and the offender is invisible in an editor. The decode
            # above is latin-1, so one character IS one byte and the code point
            # printed here is the byte the file holds.
            faults.append(
                f"  {csv_label} line {index}: {rule} at column {column}: "
                f"[{shown_character(character)}] (U+{ord(character):04X}): the escaper here "
                f"doubles apostrophes and nothing else, so any other byte would be a SQL "
                f"literal whose meaning depends on the server's standard_conforming_strings"
            )
            continue
        records.append((index, line, next(csv.reader([line]))))
    return records


def read_csv_with_header(path, label, expected_header, source_name, faults):
    """Every record of a source CSV BELOW its header, with the preamble both of
    this script's two source files need done identically.

    ONE helper and not two copies, because the preamble is four decisions and
    each one of them is a judgement this file only wants to make once. It was
    written twice -- once in main() for deadlines.csv, once inside
    read_link_status() for link-status.csv -- and the reasoning comments lived
    beside only one of them, so the second copy read as mechanical and would have
    been "simplified" by the next reader. The decisions are:

      * LINE 1 ITSELF WAS UNREADABLE -- a CR from a CRLF checkout is the common
        case, and it makes EVERY line of the file a byte fault. Those faults are
        reported and the header is NOT diagnosed: comparing a header this run
        could not decode would be a second, wrong diagnosis of the same file.
      * A FILE WITH NO LINES AT ALL is refused rather than read as a corpus with
        no rows: there is no header to agree with, so nothing below could be
        validated against anything.
      * THE HEADER IS REFUSED BEFORE ANY ROW IS READ, and it is its OWN
        diagnosis: a file whose columns are in a different order is not a file
        with bad rows, and reporting both would be a second diagnosis of a corpus
        this run never read past line 1. A header is never read POSITIONALLY --
        a reordered column would silently move an archive url into a source url.
      * THE EXPECTED/FOUND PAIR IS PRINTED, on three lines, because "the header
        is wrong" without the two strings costs the operator a diff by eye.

    `source_name` names the file in the header refusal ("deadlines CSV",
    "link-status CSV"), because this script reads two and a bare "the header is
    not the header this generator expects" would not say which.

    ONLY THE FAULTS THIS CALL PRODUCED ARE PRINTED AND COUNTED. `faults` is the
    run's one shared list, and this helper reads BOTH source files: a byte fault
    in deadlines.csv is already in it by the time link-status.csv is read, so a
    refusal counting the whole list would report deadlines.csv's faults, and
    their number, under link-status.csv's name."""
    already = len(faults)
    records = read_rows(path, label, faults)
    mine = faults[already:]

    if mine and (not records or records[0][0] != 1):
        for fault in mine:
            log_error(fault)
        fatal(f"[{len(mine)}] fault(s) in [{label}]; nothing was written")

    if not records:
        fatal(f"[{label}] has no lines at all; nothing was written")

    header_line, header_text, _header_cells = records[0]
    if header_text != expected_header:
        log_error(f"the {source_name} header is not the header this generator expects.")
        log_error(f"  expected: [{expected_header}]")
        log_error(f"  found:    [{header_text}]")
        fatal(f"refused [{label}] at line [{header_line}]; nothing was written")

    return records[1:]


def read_link_status(path, label, faults):
    """The committed sweep, as {url: (url_status, url_checked_on)}.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL: RFC 220's
    require-a-check-on-every-external-document.sql dropped
    external_documents.url_status's DEFAULT and made url_checked_on NOT NULL, so
    a generated seed that names only `url` cannot be applied to a migrated
    database. Evidence is now part of storing a citation, and this is where this
    corpus keeps it.

    IT IS A SOURCE FILE, not an artifact: an operator sweeps the cited urls with
    bin/check-link, applies this repo's reading of the codes -- 2xx found,
    404/410 not_found, everything else unknown WITH the date we asked -- and
    commits the answer for a human to review in a diff, exactly like
    deadlines.csv.

    EVERY ROW CARRIES A DATE, which is stricter than the old table CHECK and is
    the rule this file needs: `unknown` WITH a date is a check that ran and got
    no usable answer, while a url ABSENT from a file that is the record of a
    sweep is a url nobody asked about -- which is the one thing RFC 220 made
    unstorable."""
    if not path.is_file():
        fatal(
            f"[{label}] is not in the tree, so the urls this corpus cites carry no evidence "
            f"and a generated seed could not be applied: external_documents.url_status has no "
            f"default and url_checked_on is NOT NULL (RFC 220). Sweep the cited urls with "
            f"bin/check-link and commit the answer there. Nothing was written"
        )

    rows = read_csv_with_header(path, label, EXPECTED_LINK_STATUS_HEADER, "link-status CSV", faults)

    swept = {}
    first_seen = {}
    for line, _text, cells in rows:
        if len(cells) != 3:
            faults.append(f"  {label} line {line}: [{len(cells)}] columns, expected [3]")
            continue
        url, status, checked_on = cells

        # MIRRORS external_documents_url_shape_check
        if not URL_SHAPE.match(url):
            faults.append(f"  {label} line {line}: url [{url}] is not an http or https url")
        # MIRRORS link_status_check, the domain every url-bearing table shares.
        if status not in LINK_STATUSES:
            faults.append(
                f"  {label} line {line}: url_status [{status}] is not one of "
                f"[{', '.join(LINK_STATUSES)}]"
            )
        if not is_iso_date(checked_on):
            faults.append(
                f"  {label} line {line}: url_checked_on [{checked_on}] is not a real yyyy-mm-dd "
                f"day; a swept url carries the day it was asked about (RFC 220)"
            )
        # One url, one answer. Two rows for one address is two statuses for one
        # external_documents row, and the seed would then depend on which one
        # this reader happened to keep.
        if url in first_seen:
            faults.append(
                f"  {label} line {line}: url [{url}] is already swept at line "
                f"[{first_seen[url]}]; one url carries one answer"
            )
            continue
        first_seen[url] = line
        swept[url] = (status, checked_on)
    return swept


def validate_and_render(records, csv_label, faults, published, swept, link_label):
    """Every row, checked against every rule, and rendered.

    EVERY row is checked and EVERY fault is collected: a validator that stopped
    at the first offender would turn a five-line fix into five runs. The return
    value is a list of rendered rows; the caller writes nothing while `faults`
    is non-empty."""
    rendered = []
    first_seen = {}
    columns = EXPECTED_HEADER.split(",")

    for line, _text, cells in records:
        faults_before = len(faults)

        def fail(message, line=line):
            faults.append(f"  {csv_label} line {line}: {message}")

        if len(cells) != len(columns):
            fail(f"[{len(cells)}] columns, expected [{len(columns)}]")
            continue

        (
            state,
            award_year_text,
            program,
            event,
            kind,
            deadline_date,
            as_published,
            publisher,
            source_url,
            archive_url,
            checked_on,
            expires_on,
        ) = cells

        # MIRRORS fafsa_filing_deadlines_state_format_check
        #
        # The SHAPE, exactly as the table checks it -- and EMPTY is legal,
        # because the column is nullable and its NULL means FEDERAL. The federal
        # rows are typed with an empty state cell and are a jurisdiction like any
        # other everywhere else.
        state_shape_ok = state == "" or bool(STATE_SHAPE.match(state))
        if not state_shape_ok:
            fail(
                f"state [{state}] is not a two-letter uppercase USPS code, and is not empty; "
                f"an EMPTY cell is the FEDERAL row"
            )
        # MEMBERSHIP, which no CHECK constraint can state and no foreign key
        # enforces yet: ^[A-Z]{2}$ admits 676 strings and 59 of them name a
        # jurisdiction db/data/codebooks.json publishes.
        elif state != "" and state not in published:
            fail(
                f"state [{state}] is not one of the [{len(published)}] USPS codes "
                f"db/data/codebooks.json publishes; a two-letter shape is not a jurisdiction"
            )

        # MIRRORS academic_year_check
        #
        # The `academic_year` DOMAIN (0087, RFC 170 D14) is a SMALLINT between
        # 2000 and 2100. A domain violation is a migration that fails at apply
        # time exactly like a table CHECK.
        award_year = None
        if not re.match(r"^[0-9]{4}$", award_year_text):
            fail(
                f"award_year [{award_year_text}] is not a four-digit year; it is a START "
                f"year, so the 2027-28 FAFSA is 2027"
            )
        elif not AWARD_YEAR_MIN <= int(award_year_text) <= AWARD_YEAR_MAX:
            fail(
                f"award_year [{award_year_text}] is outside the academic_year domain "
                f"([{AWARD_YEAR_MIN}]..[{AWARD_YEAR_MAX}])"
            )
        else:
            award_year = int(award_year_text)

        # MIRRORS fafsa_filing_deadlines_event_check
        if event not in EVENTS:
            fail(f"event [{event}] is not one of [{', '.join(EVENTS)}]")

        # MIRRORS fafsa_filing_deadlines_kind_check
        if kind not in KINDS:
            fail(f"deadline_kind [{kind}] is not one of [{', '.join(KINDS)}]")

        # MIRRORS fafsa_filing_deadlines_date_iff_kind_check
        #
        # A date exists exactly when the kind bears one. This is what stops a
        # 'wording' row quietly growing a date somebody guessed -- the invented
        # fact this table exists to prevent -- and stops a 'date' row with
        # nothing in it reaching the page as a blank cell.
        if kind in KINDS and (deadline_date != "") != (kind == "date"):
            if kind == "date":
                fail("deadline_kind is [date] and deadline_date is empty: a dated row names its day")
            else:
                fail(
                    f"deadline_kind is [{kind}] and deadline_date is [{deadline_date}]: only a "
                    f"[date] row carries a day, and a rule the publisher stated in words is never "
                    f"flattened into a date we chose"
                )

        if deadline_date != "" and not is_iso_date(deadline_date):
            fail(f"deadline_date [{deadline_date}] is not a real yyyy-mm-dd date")

        # MIRRORS fafsa_filing_deadlines_opening_is_dated_check
        #
        # A form that opens does open. 'none' is a statement about a deadline a
        # publisher does not set; a form_opens row saying it would render as
        # "the FAFSA does not open".
        if event == "form_opens" and kind == "none":
            fail(
                "a [form_opens] row is never kind [none]: 'none' says the publisher sets no "
                "deadline, which is not a thing to say about the day the form opens"
            )

        # MIRRORS fafsa_filing_deadlines_date_in_cycle_check
        #
        # [award_year - 1 July 1, award_year + 1 Dec 31]. THE LOWER BOUND IS THE
        # HALF THAT EARNS THIS CHECK: it refuses LAST cycle's date typed under
        # this cycle's key, which is the one mistake that makes this table worse
        # than no table, because a family acts on it. It is `award_year - 1` and
        # never `award_year - 2` -- a window probed with a date two cycles back
        # would pass a generator that had the bound wrong by a year.
        if award_year is not None and deadline_date != "" and is_iso_date(deadline_date):
            opens = cycle_opens_on(award_year)
            closes = cycle_closes_on(award_year)
            if not opens <= date.fromisoformat(deadline_date) <= closes:
                fail(
                    f"deadline_date [{deadline_date}] is outside the [{award_year}] cycle's own "
                    f"window [{opens}]..[{closes}]: a date from a NEIGHBOURING cycle typed under "
                    f"this one's award_year is a date a family would act on"
                )

        # MIRRORS fafsa_filing_deadlines_program_nonempty_check
        #
        # Trimmed AND non-empty, and the trim is the load-bearing half:
        # `program` is in the natural key, so 'Cal Grant ' makes one programme
        # two rows and the public page prints it twice.
        if program != program.strip() or program == "":
            fail(f"program [{program}] must be trimmed and non-empty; it is part of the natural key")

        # MIRRORS fafsa_filing_deadlines_deadline_as_published_nonempty_check
        if as_published != as_published.strip() or as_published == "":
            fail(
                f"deadline_as_published [{as_published}] must be trimmed and non-empty; every row "
                f"says what the publisher published, whatever its kind"
            )

        # MIRRORS fafsa_filing_deadlines_publisher_nonempty_check
        if publisher != publisher.strip() or publisher == "":
            fail(
                f"publisher [{publisher}] must be trimmed and non-empty; the public page renders "
                f"it as the link text, so without it the row cites a bare url"
            )

        # MIRRORS external_documents_url_shape_check
        #
        # http IS ACCEPTED beside https, the ruling the sibling corpus records
        # (Ian, 2026-09-14: prefer https, do not block insecure). An agency page
        # served over plain http is still the agency's own page. A citation with
        # NO url is refused outright -- source_document_id is NOT NULL, and a row
        # that cites nothing is the uncitable row this corpus exists to prevent.
        if not URL_SHAPE.match(source_url):
            fail(
                f"source_url [{source_url}] is not an http or https url: a row that cites nothing "
                f"is the uncitable row this corpus exists to prevent"
            )
        if archive_url != "" and not URL_SHAPE.match(archive_url):
            fail(f"archive_url [{archive_url}] is not an http or https url")

        # A CITATION CARRIES EVIDENCE, OR IT CANNOT BE STORED (RFC 220). Both
        # urls, because an archived address is a document like any other and is
        # stored in the same table with the same NOT NULL columns. The fault
        # names the DEADLINE line, which is the line a human edits; the sweep
        # file is named beside it so the way out is in the message.
        for cited in (source_url, archive_url):
            if cited == "" or not URL_SHAPE.match(cited):
                continue
            if cited not in swept:
                fail(
                    f"url [{cited}] has no row in [{link_label}], so this seed could not state "
                    f"its url_status or url_checked_on -- and external_documents has no default "
                    f"for either (RFC 220). Sweep it with bin/check-link and add its row"
                )

        # MIRRORS fafsa_filing_deadlines_archive_is_not_the_source_check
        #
        # An archive of a page may not BE that page: a page told to offer both
        # links would show one url twice.
        if archive_url != "" and archive_url == source_url:
            fail(
                f"archive_url [{archive_url}] is the source_url: an archive of a page is not that "
                f"page, and a row carrying both would print one link twice"
            )

        checked_ok = is_iso_date(checked_on)
        if not checked_ok:
            fail(f"checked_on [{checked_on}] is not a real yyyy-mm-dd date")
        if not is_iso_date(expires_on):
            fail(f"expires_on [{expires_on}] is not a real yyyy-mm-dd date")

        # MIRRORS fafsa_filing_deadlines_expiry_after_checked_check
        if checked_ok and is_iso_date(expires_on) and expires_on <= checked_on:
            fail(f"expires_on [{expires_on}] is not after checked_on [{checked_on}]")

        # MIRRORS fafsa_filing_deadlines_checked_in_or_after_cycle_check
        #
        # The same lower bound as the date window, one bound only, because a
        # human may legitimately re-read a page years later to confirm it. An
        # agency publishes a cycle's deadline in the months before that cycle
        # opens, never a year and a half before -- so an earlier checked_on is a
        # mistyped award_year, not a diligent reader.
        if award_year is not None and checked_ok:
            opens = cycle_opens_on(award_year)
            if date.fromisoformat(checked_on) < opens:
                fail(
                    f"checked_on [{checked_on}] is before the [{award_year}] cycle opened "
                    f"[{opens}]: a page cannot have said this before it could have said it, so "
                    f"the award_year is mistyped"
                )

        # At least one fault on this row. Nothing will be written anyway, so the
        # rendering below is skipped rather than run over cells that failed --
        # and the duplicate-key check is skipped with it, because a row that is
        # not a row cannot own a key.
        if len(faults) > faults_before:
            continue

        # MIRRORS fafsa_filing_deadlines_natural_key
        #
        # (award_year, state, program, event) with NULLS NOT DISTINCT -- and the
        # duplicate rule is PER READING DAY, for the reason written at the top of
        # this file: two rows with one key on one day would land in one
        # `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE`, which Postgres refuses outright,
        # while the SAME key on a LATER day is the correction mechanism itself.
        key = (checked_on, award_year, state, program, event)
        if key in first_seen:
            fail(
                f"duplicate (award_year, state, program, event) "
                f"([{award_year}], [{jurisdiction_label(state)}], [{program}], [{event}]) read on "
                f"[{checked_on}], first seen at line [{first_seen[key]}]: one reading day may "
                f"carry one row per key, and a CORRECTION is a re-read with a LATER checked_on"
            )
            continue
        first_seen[key] = line

        # RENDERED FROM SEED_COLUMNS, never from a hand-written f-string: the
        # column ORDER is stated once, and the INSERT's column list above the
        # tuples and the reader's key positions come from the same tuple.
        tuple_sql = render_tuple(
            {
                "award_year": award_year,
                "state": state,
                "program": program,
                "event": event,
                "kind": kind,
                "deadline_date": deadline_date,
                "as_published": as_published,
                "publisher": publisher,
                "source_url": source_url,
                "archive_url": archive_url,
                "checked_on": checked_on,
                "expires_on": expires_on,
            }
        )
        rendered.append(
            {
                "line": line,
                "checked_on": checked_on,
                "award_year": award_year,
                "state": state,
                "program": program,
                "event": event,
                "urls": [url for url in (source_url, archive_url) if url != ""],
                "tuple": tuple_sql,
            }
        )
    return rendered


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The generated seeds
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# THE ROW ORDER OF A SEED, DECIDED ONCE AND WRITTEN DOWN. A generated file must
# be byte-identical across runs or `-n` refuses the tree it has just
# regenerated, so the order cannot be "whatever the CSV happened to say".
#
# It is: THE FEDERAL ROWS FIRST, then by state, then award_year, program, event.
# Federal first because the federal dates are the ones that apply to every
# family and the file reads top-down; the rest alphabetically, because a reviewer
# looking for Ohio in a diff looks where Ohio would be. The CSV's own row order
# is deliberately NOT used: re-sorting the authored file would then rewrite a
# landed seed.
def sort_key(row):
    return (0 if row["state"] == "" else 1, row["state"], row["award_year"], row["program"], row["event"])


def render_seed(day, rows, csv_label, swept):
    """One reading day's whole file, as text."""
    rows = sorted(rows, key=sort_key)

    # EVERY distinct url of the day, source and archive alike -- an archived
    # address is a document like any other, and a row citing one the upsert never
    # inserted would violate the foreign key at apply time.
    #
    # SORTED, and not first-seen as the sibling does it, for the same reason the
    # ROW order above is derived: the block must be stable whatever order the CSV
    # lists the rows in, so that re-sorting the authored file can never rewrite a
    # LANDED seed. A first-seen order makes the generated bytes depend on the
    # CSV's own line order, which nothing forbids a human from changing.
    urls = sorted({url for row in rows for url in row["urls"]})

    lines = [
        "-- GENERATED FILE -- do not edit.",
        "--",
        f"-- Written by bin/fafsa-deadline-seed from {csv_label}.",
        "-- The CSV is the source a human edits and reviews in a diff; this file is an",
        "-- artifact of it. To change a row, change the CSV and re-run the script.",
        "--",
        f"-- Read on {day}: {len(rows)} FAFSA filing deadline row(s), each with the publisher's",
        "-- own page it was read from and the day a human read it. The corpus is append-only,",
        "-- so a CORRECTION is a re-read -- which carries a new checked_on and therefore lands",
        "-- as a NEW seed upserting on the natural key, never as an edit to this one.",
        "--",
        "-- Each row cites its documents by foreign key into external_documents (RFC 213). The",
        "-- urls are upserted FIRST, and each id is SUBSELECTED by url, because the id is a",
        "-- uuidv7 the database mints and a generated file cannot know it.",
        "--",
        "-- Each url carries the sweep bin/check-link answered for it: url_status has no default",
        "-- and url_checked_on is NOT NULL (RFC 220), so a citation states its evidence or it",
        "-- cannot be stored. The answers are committed beside the corpus in link-status.csv.",
        "",
        "INSERT INTO external_documents (url, url_status, url_checked_on)",
        "VALUES",
    ]
    for index, url in enumerate(urls):
        separator = "" if index == len(urls) - 1 else ","
        status, checked_on = swept[url]
        lines.append(
            f"{DOCUMENT_INDENT}({sql_quoted(url)}, {sql_quoted(status)}, "
            f"{sql_date(checked_on)}){separator}"
        )
    # DO NOTHING and never DO UPDATE: a url this seed cites may already have been
    # swept -- by bin/check-link for another corpus, or on a later day than this
    # file records -- and re-stating it must not reset url_status to an older
    # answer or to "we never asked" (RFC 182, RFC 213, RFC 220).
    lines.extend(
        [
            "ON CONFLICT (url) DO NOTHING;",
            "",
            DEADLINE_INSERT,
            *insert_column_lines(),
            VALUES_LINE,
        ]
    )
    for index, row in enumerate(rows):
        separator = "" if index == len(rows) - 1 else ","
        lines.append(f"{row['tuple']}{separator}")
    lines.extend(
        [
            f"{CONFLICT_PREFIX}(award_year, state, program, event) DO UPDATE SET",
            "    deadline_kind         = EXCLUDED.deadline_kind,",
            "    deadline_date         = EXCLUDED.deadline_date,",
            "    deadline_as_published = EXCLUDED.deadline_as_published,",
            "    publisher             = EXCLUDED.publisher,",
            "    source_document_id    = EXCLUDED.source_document_id,",
            "    archive_document_id   = EXCLUDED.archive_document_id,",
            "    checked_on            = EXCLUDED.checked_on,",
            "    expires_on            = EXCLUDED.expires_on;",
        ]
    )
    return "\n".join(lines) + "\n"


def unreadable_seed_message(path, reason):
    """The refusal every unreadable GENERATED seed gets, in one sentence, because
    the operator's fix is the same one whatever the line looked like."""
    return (
        f"[{path.name}] is a generated seed this script cannot read back: {reason}. The "
        f"append-only rule cannot be checked against a file whose rows cannot be read, so "
        f"nothing was written. A generated seed is never hand-edited: restore it from git, or "
        f"-- if it has NOT LANDED yet -- delete it and its line in ORDER and re-run"
    )


def deadline_tuple_lines(path, body):
    """Every line of a generated seed's `fafsa_filing_deadlines` VALUES block, as
    (1-based line number, text) -- and a REFUSAL for anything in that block that
    is not a tuple.

    THE GUARD MAY NOT FAIL OPEN, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS A BLOCK READER AND NOT A
    LINE FILTER. It used to `continue` past every line it did not recognise, on
    the argument that a deadline tuple is the only thing indented TUPLE_INDENT --
    true of a file this generator wrote today, and beside the point, because the
    reason the guard exists is the file a HUMAN touched. A re-indented tuple, a
    `);` terminator or a `--` comment made that row INVISIBLE, and an invisible
    landed row can then be deleted from the CSV with no fault reported at all:
    the "dropped" branch never fires for a row the reader never saw.

    The structure IS known -- this script wrote it -- so it is read as structure:
    the one `INSERT INTO fafsa_filing_deadlines`, its column list (which must be
    the one this generator writes, or the fields below would be read at the wrong
    positions), its `VALUES`, and then tuples until `ON CONFLICT`. Anything else
    in there is a fault naming the file and the line."""
    lines = body.splitlines()
    starts = [index for index, text in enumerate(lines) if text == DEADLINE_INSERT]
    if len(starts) != 1:
        fatal(
            unreadable_seed_message(
                path,
                f"it holds [{len(starts)}] `{DEADLINE_INSERT}` statement(s) and a generated "
                f"seed holds exactly one",
            )
        )

    index = starts[0] + 1
    while index < len(lines) and lines[index] != VALUES_LINE:
        index += 1
    if index == len(lines):
        fatal(
            unreadable_seed_message(
                path, f"its `{DEADLINE_INSERT}` is followed by no `{VALUES_LINE}` line"
            )
        )

    columns = lines[starts[0] + 1 : index]
    if columns != insert_column_lines():
        fatal(
            unreadable_seed_message(
                path,
                f"its column list is not the one this generator writes, so its tuples cannot "
                f"be read by position (found {columns}, this generator writes "
                f"{insert_column_lines()})",
            )
        )

    collected = []
    index += 1
    while index < len(lines) and not lines[index].startswith(CONFLICT_PREFIX):
        collected.append((index + 1, lines[index]))
        index += 1
    if index == len(lines):
        fatal(
            unreadable_seed_message(
                path, f"its `{VALUES_LINE}` block is not terminated by an `{CONFLICT_PREFIX}` line"
            )
        )
    if not collected:
        fatal(unreadable_seed_message(path, f"its `{VALUES_LINE}` block carries no tuple"))
    return collected


def read_prior_seeds(schema_dir):
    """Every tuple every ALREADY-GENERATED seed in the schema directory carries,
    keyed by (reading day, award_year, state, program, event).

    Read as DATA, and the whole rendered tuple is kept: "rewritten" means any
    cell of it changed, and comparing the renderings is the one check that needs
    no column list. The tuples themselves come from deadline_tuple_lines above,
    which REFUSES anything in the VALUES block it cannot read rather than
    skipping it, and the natural key's positions come from SEED_COLUMNS -- the
    same tuple the writer renders from."""
    prior = {}
    for path in sorted(schema_dir.glob("seed-fafsa-filing-deadlines-*.sql")):
        match = SEED_NAME_PATTERN.match(path.name)
        # NAMED, not skipped: the glob has already said this file belongs to this
        # corpus, so a name the pattern then refuses (`-2026-9-1.sql`,
        # `-2026-09-14.bak.sql`) is a generated seed the guard would be blind to.
        if match is None:
            fatal(
                f"[{path}] is in the schema directory and its name is not "
                f"[{SEED_NAME_TEMPLATE % 'yyyy-mm-dd'}], so the reading day it holds cannot be "
                f"read and the append-only rule cannot be checked; nothing was written. Rename "
                f"it, or move it out of the schema directory"
            )
        day = match.group(1)
        try:
            body = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as error:
            fatal(
                f"could not read the generated seed [{path}] "
                f"([{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]); the append-only rule cannot be checked, "
                f"so nothing was written"
            )
        for number, line in deadline_tuple_lines(path, body):
            # ONE refusal for every way this line can be unreadable, because the
            # operator's fix is the same one either way. A landed seed is a
            # GENERATED file nobody should have touched, but a hand-edited one is
            # exactly what turns up here -- and an unguarded int() on its first
            # field would answer with a python traceback naming no file, which is
            # the outcome the named fatal beside it exists to prevent.
            unreadable = unreadable_seed_message(
                path, f"line {number} of its VALUES block is not a tuple this script wrote: [{line}]"
            )
            fields = tuple_fields(line)
            if fields is None:
                fatal(unreadable)
            # EXACTLY the twelve columns, never "at least four". A line carrying
            # five fields, or thirteen, would be read positionally all the same,
            # so a hand-edited seed that dropped a column would still produce a
            # plausible key and the guard would report nothing.
            if len(fields) != len(SEED_COLUMNS):
                fatal(
                    unreadable_seed_message(
                        path,
                        f"line {number} of its VALUES block carries [{len(fields)}] field(s) and "
                        f"this generator writes [{len(SEED_COLUMNS)}]: [{line}]",
                    )
                )
            year_index, state_index, program_index, event_index = KEY_INDEXES
            try:
                award_year = int(fields[year_index])
            except ValueError as error:
                fatal(f"{unreadable} ([{type(error).__name__}] [{error}])")
            key = (
                day,
                award_year,
                # SQL NULL comes back as None, which is what the federal
                # jurisdiction IS here -- the empty string is the CSV's spelling
                # of it and never reaches this key. `or ""` used to fold NULL, a
                # literal '' and any other falsy read into one jurisdiction.
                sql_unquoted(fields[state_index]),
                sql_unquoted(fields[program_index]),
                sql_unquoted(fields[event_index]),
            )
            prior[key] = (path.name, line.rstrip(","))
    return prior


def check_append_only(prior, rendered, csv_label, faults):
    """The corpus is append-only, CHECKED and not assumed.

    A row a landed generated seed already carries may be re-stated identically --
    this script is re-run all the time and must be idempotent -- and may be
    neither REWRITTEN nor DROPPED. Both halves need a whole pass to know, so both
    are here rather than in the per-row loop.

    Returns the seed FILES this guard complained about, so the caller can print
    the escape below ONCE PER FILE. A corpus-wide change touches every row, and a
    hundred and twenty-eight copies of the same paragraph is a refusal nobody
    reads to the end of."""
    offending_files = set()
    generated = {}
    for row in rendered:
        generated[
            (
                row["checked_on"],
                row["award_year"],
                # The federal jurisdiction is None on BOTH sides -- the value the
                # nullable column carries -- so a key rendered from the CSV and a
                # key read back out of a landed seed are the same python value.
                jurisdiction(row["state"]),
                row["program"],
                row["event"],
            )
        ] = row

    # Sorted through a key that spells the federal None as "", because None and a
    # USPS code do not compare in python and the two sort side by side here.
    for key, (filename, landed_tuple) in sorted(
        prior.items(), key=lambda item: tuple("" if part is None else part for part in item[0])
    ):
        day, award_year, state, program, event = key
        state = jurisdiction_label(state)
        row = generated.get(key)
        if row is None:
            faults.append(
                f"  {csv_label}: (award_year, state, program, event) ([{award_year}], "
                f"[{state}], [{program}], [{event}]) read on [{day}] is in the "
                f"generated seed [{filename}] and is no longer in the CSV. The corpus is "
                f"append-only: a landed migration is replayed on every db-reset and cannot lose a "
                f"row. A CORRECTION is a re-read with a LATER checked_on, appended beside this row."
            )
            offending_files.add(filename)
            continue
        if row["tuple"] != landed_tuple:
            faults.append(
                f"  {csv_label} line {row['line']}: (award_year, state, program, event) "
                f"([{award_year}], [{state}], [{program}], [{event}]) read on [{day}] "
                f"is already in the generated seed [{filename}] and this row rewrites it. The "
                f"corpus is append-only: a correction is a re-read, which carries a new checked_on "
                f"and lands as a NEW seed, not as an edit to a landed one.\n"
                + tuple_difference(landed_tuple, row["tuple"])
            )
            offending_files.add(filename)
    return sorted(offending_files)


def tuple_difference(landed_tuple, generated_tuple):
    """WHICH CELL MOVED, by column name, and not merely that one did.

    This guard has both renderings in hand, and flattening them into "this row
    rewrites it" left the operator to open a landed migration and diff twelve SQL
    fields by eye -- once per offending row, on a corpus-wide edit. The columns
    are SEED_COLUMNS', so the names printed here are the table's own. Both whole
    tuples are the fallback, for the line this reader cannot split."""
    landed = tuple_fields(landed_tuple)
    generated = tuple_fields(generated_tuple)
    if (
        landed is not None
        and generated is not None
        and len(landed) == len(generated) == len(SEED_COLUMNS)
    ):
        differences = [
            f"      {name}: landed [{before}], generated [{after}]"
            for name, before, after in zip(SEED_COLUMN_NAMES, landed, generated)
            if before != after
        ]
        if differences:
            return "\n".join(differences)
    return (
        f"      landed:    {landed_tuple.strip()}\n"
        f"      generated: {generated_tuple.strip()}"
    )


# THE ESCAPE, WRITTEN DOWN WHERE THE REFUSAL IS READ. The guard above is right
# about a LANDED seed and unhelpful about one that is still being AUTHORED -- a
# seed this very run wrote ten minutes ago, uncommitted, which the next run then
# treats as history. That happened in the RFC 223 authoring run itself: one
# changed `expires_on` in the corpus refused all 128 rows, and the way out had to
# be worked out rather than read. It is one sentence, so it is printed rather
# than worked out.
#
# The refusal itself does NOT change. Deleting a seed that has landed is the one
# thing this corpus may never do, so the escape names the condition ("has NOT
# landed yet") before it names the command.
def append_only_escape(filename):
    return (
        f"  [{filename}] is the generated seed those rows are in. If it has NOT LANDED yet -- "
        f"you are still authoring this reading -- delete it and its line in ORDER, then re-run. "
        f"The guard is about a LANDED seed: once a migration is in main it is replayed on every "
        f"db-reset and may never change."
    )


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# db/schema/ORDER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE HONEST MINIMUM, AND IT IS SAID OUT LOUD. bin/common owns what ORDER is,
# what a legal migration filename is and what counts as a disagreement, and the
# bash siblings ASK it (schema_order_scan / schema_order_lists) rather than
# answering with their own `grep -Fxq`. A python script cannot source bin/common,
# and shelling out to bash to ask one question would put a second process in the
# middle of a file this script is about to append to. So the minimum is
# implemented here, and the minimum is exactly this:
#
#   * every line is NORMALISED -- trailing whitespace and a CR from a CRLF
#     checkout stripped -- before it is compared. That is the defect bin/prompt-
#     seed records as already shipped: `grep -Fxq` answers "is there a line whose
#     BYTES are exactly this", so a line carrying a trailing space reads as
#     ABSENT, the append writes a SECOND line for one migration, and bin/db-
#     migrate refuses two steps later a corpus this script had just called
#     correct;
#   * a NORMALISED DUPLICATE anywhere in ORDER, or a missing ORDER, is
#     EXIT_SCHEMA_ORDER_DRIFT (9) and not something to append to -- the same
#     answer bin/common's schema_order_file_is_corrupt gives, and the same
#     instruction: fix it and re-run, `bin/db-status` reports the same corpus
#     without refusing it.
#
# WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO IS PLACE THE LINE. It APPENDS, at the end,
# and /ship's `.prime/agent/skills/ship/scripts/ship-order` owns the final
# PLACEMENT at land time -- a seed must apply after the table it fills, and where
# exactly that is depends on what else landed while this branch was open.


def normalised_order_lines(order_file):
    try:
        body = order_file.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as error:
        fatal(
            f"could not read [{order_file}] ([{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]); a generated "
            f"seed nothing declares would never be applied, so nothing was written",
            status=EXIT_SCHEMA_ORDER_DRIFT,
        )
    return [line.rstrip().rstrip("\r") for line in body.splitlines()]


def assert_order_is_appendable(order_file):
    """Refuses unless a line can be appended to ORDER safely. A GUARD, and only
    a guard: it returns nothing, so a caller that also wants the lines asks
    normalised_order_lines for them and cannot mistake state for a check."""
    if not order_file.is_file():
        fatal(
            f"[{order_file}] does not exist, so a generated seed could never be applied; "
            f"nothing was written",
            status=EXIT_SCHEMA_ORDER_DRIFT,
        )
    lines = normalised_order_lines(order_file)
    counts = Counter(line for line in lines if line != "")
    duplicates = sorted(name for name, count in counts.items() if count > 1)
    if duplicates:
        fatal(
            f"[{order_file}] cannot be appended to safely: it lists "
            f"{', '.join(f'[{name}]' for name in duplicates)} more than once. Fix it and re-run. "
            f"`bin/db-status` reports the same corpus without refusing it",
            status=EXIT_SCHEMA_ORDER_DRIFT,
        )


def append_to_order(order_file, name):
    try:
        body = order_file.read_bytes()
        prefix = b"" if body == b"" or body.endswith(b"\n") else b"\n"
        with order_file.open("ab") as handle:
            handle.write(prefix + name.encode("ascii") + b"\n")
    except OSError as error:
        fatal(
            f"could not append [{name}] to [{order_file}] ([{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]); "
            f"the seed is in the schema directory and nothing declares it, so it would never be "
            f"applied. Add the line by hand or re-run"
        )


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# main
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def resolve_paths(options):
    csv_path = Path(options.get("-c", str(DEFAULT_CSV)))
    schema_arg = Path(options.get("-d", str(DEFAULT_SCHEMA_DIR)))

    if not csv_path.is_file():
        fatal(
            f"deadlines CSV not found [{csv_path}]",
            status=EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE,
        )
    if csv_path.stat().st_size == 0:
        fatal(
            f"deadlines CSV is empty [{csv_path}]; a file with no header is not a corpus this "
            f"validator can read",
            status=EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE,
        )
    if not schema_arg.is_dir():
        fatal(
            f"schema directory not found [{schema_arg}]"
            + ("" if not schema_arg.exists() else ", and what is there is not a directory"),
            status=EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE,
        )
    return csv_path.resolve(), schema_arg.resolve()


def label_for(path):
    """The path as a message says it: relative to the repo root where it is
    under it, and absolute otherwise (a harness fixture in a temp tree)."""
    try:
        return str(path.relative_to(REPO_ROOT))
    except ValueError:
        return str(path)


def main(argv):
    options = parse_short_options(argv, GETOPT_SPEC, __doc__, SCRIPT_NAME)
    dry_run = "-n" in options
    csv_path, schema_dir = resolve_paths(options)
    order_file = schema_dir / "ORDER"
    csv_label = label_for(csv_path)
    # BESIDE the authored CSV, and not behind an option of its own: the sweep is
    # a fact about THAT corpus's urls, so -c names both files.
    link_status_path = csv_path.parent / LINK_STATUS_NAME
    link_label = label_for(link_status_path)

    assert_order_is_appendable(order_file)

    faults = []
    # The byte faults, the empty file, and the header -- all four decisions are
    # read_csv_with_header's, which is also where the reasoning for each one is
    # written down, because link-status.csv needs the identical preamble.
    rows = read_csv_with_header(csv_path, csv_label, EXPECTED_HEADER, "deadlines CSV", faults)

    published = published_state_codes()
    # The sweep is read BEFORE the rows, so a row whose url nobody asked about is
    # a fault named by its own line rather than a migration that fails at apply
    # time in somebody else's db-reset (RFC 220).
    swept = read_link_status(link_status_path, link_label, faults)
    rendered = validate_and_render(rows, csv_label, faults, published, swept, link_label)

    if not faults and not rendered:
        fatal(f"[{csv_label}] has a header and no rows; nothing was written")

    # A CORPUS THAT DID NOT PARSE IS NOT COMPARED WITH WHAT LANDED, and this is a
    # correctness rule rather than an optimisation. validate_and_render SKIPS a
    # faulty row, so check_append_only cannot see it: one typo in a state cell
    # makes the landed tuple look like a row the CSV DROPPED, and that second,
    # FALSE fault prints the escape -- which tells the operator to delete a
    # landed migration, the one action this corpus may never take. The
    # append-only question is only answerable about a corpus every row of which
    # was read, so it is asked only then.
    rewritten_seeds = []
    if not faults:
        rewritten_seeds = check_append_only(read_prior_seeds(schema_dir), rendered, csv_label, faults)

    if faults:
        for fault in faults:
            log_error(fault)
        # ONCE PER FILE, after the rows, so the way out is the last thing read
        # and is not repeated once per offending row.
        for filename in rewritten_seeds:
            log_error(append_only_escape(filename))
        fatal(f"[{len(faults)}] fault(s) in [{csv_label}]; nothing was written")

    days = {}
    for row in rendered:
        days.setdefault(row["checked_on"], []).append(row)

    order_lines = normalised_order_lines(order_file)
    changed = False

    for day in sorted(days):
        name = SEED_NAME_TEMPLATE % day
        body = render_seed(day, days[day], csv_label, swept)
        target = schema_dir / name

        current = None
        if target.is_file():
            try:
                current = target.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
            except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as error:
                fatal(
                    f"could not read [{target}] to compare it with what [{csv_label}] generates "
                    f"([{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]); nothing has been written"
                )

        if current == body:
            log_info(f"{SCRIPT_NAME}: [{name}] is already what this corpus generates.")
        else:
            changed = True
            if dry_run:
                log_error(f"{SCRIPT_NAME}: [{target}] is not what [{csv_label}] generates.")
                log_error("  Run: nix develop -c bin/fafsa-deadline-seed")
            else:
                # ATOMICALLY, through bin/pyfunctions.py's shared writer: the
                # body is staged beside the target and renamed into place, so an
                # interrupted or out-of-space run leaves the PREVIOUS file
                # standing rather than a truncated one. What is written here is a
                # MIGRATION, replayed from the top on every db-reset, and half an
                # INSERT is a corpus nobody can apply. write_atomically refuses
                # through this script's own channel, so there is no OSError to
                # catch here.
                write_atomically([(target, body.encode("utf-8"))], SCRIPT_NAME)
                log_info(f"{SCRIPT_NAME}: wrote [{target}].")

        if name in order_lines:
            continue
        changed = True
        if dry_run:
            log_error(
                f"{SCRIPT_NAME}: [{order_file}] does not declare [{name}], so the seed would "
                f"never be applied."
            )
        else:
            append_to_order(order_file, name)
            log_info(f"{SCRIPT_NAME}: appended [{name}] to [{order_file}].")
            # The append made the list above stale, and the next day asks the
            # same question. Re-read the corpus rather than reasoning about what
            # this loop just did -- and re-assert it, because an append that
            # produced a duplicate is a corpus bin/db-migrate would refuse.
            assert_order_is_appendable(order_file)
            order_lines = normalised_order_lines(order_file)

    if dry_run and changed:
        return EXIT_SEED_WOULD_CHANGE
    log_info(f"{SCRIPT_NAME}: done.")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
